In order to use cuda you must first load the cuda module by running:
'module load cuda'

See the bridges manual, section "Using Bridges GPU Nodes" for additional details.

'autocorrect' is the program that checks whether your particles are interacting properly. If it prints an error, then your particles are not interacting properly.

Use the autograder to check your performance, you will need to submit 'auto-bridges-gpu' and then run 'python gen_gpusum.py' to general summary statistics and grade your code.

Note that 'auto-bridges-gpu' tests your GPU code against 'serial.cu'. Without any modifications, it tests the O(n^2) GPU code against the O(n^2) serial code that are already provided. 

You will need to modify either 'auto-bridges-gpu' or replace 'gpu.cu' and 'serial.cu' with their O(n) versions. You should already have a working O(n) serial code from Part 1 of this assignment.

We will be testing your code against an optimized, O(n), 'serial.cu' code.

Your write-up should give a synopsis of what you did to complete the assignment: the methods you chose, the code modifications you made and why, and how those choices shaped your results. Write it in your own words. The paper should be about a half-page to a full page in length. The document can be saved as: .txt, .doc(x), .odf, .md, .html or .pdf.

I am requiring output, error logs, compiler logs, source code, object code, scripts, etc from the HPC. Please create a HW3 directory (which you get from my Github repo) and complete all your work in that directory. When you are ready to submit the work you should tar the entire directory and structure up, and please be sure to include your name as part of the tar filename. This will help speed up grading as I will not have to make requests for more files. Also, going forward a github repo will be required and graded as part of the assignments. Most students have already added me to their github repositories.
